ABSTRACT = f"""The APC-NDG survey of October--November 2026 reports, for residents on rue de Terrebonne or within one block, how many keep a vehicle, how many have an off-street place, where the car was parked last night and how often winter parking was hard. Those are counts of respondents. This note places them against the measurements that exist of the parking they describe: the borough\\textquoteright{{}}s 2023 study (EXP), which inventoried {E['stated']['terrebonne']} on-street places on Terrebonne and {E['stated']['stubs']} on the cross-street stubs within 50 m and measured their occupancy on a Thursday and a Saturday of September--October 2022; the 2015 City LiDAR night and a daytime orthophoto, on which the cars at Terrebonne\\textquoteright{{}}s curbs were counted block by block before the 2024 lane; the 1954--55 insurance plans and present-day imagery, which give the share of buildings with a garage or driveway; the City assessment roll, which gives the dwellings; and five winters of snow-removal records. EXP found occupancy below its own 80\\,\\% threshold at every period in every sector: at 18:30--19:30 on the Thursday, {tp['terrebonne_occupied']} cars occupied {pc(tp['terrebonne_occupancy'])} of Terrebonne\\textquoteright{{}}s places, leaving {tp['terrebonne_vacant']} vacant there and {tp['stubs_vacant']} on the stubs. The 2015 night count found {CU['lidar_night_by_eye']} cars at the curbs, {pc(CU['lidar_night_over_exp_supply'])} of the 2022 supply, rising from {pc(CU['by_sector']['residential west']['lidar_night_over_exp_supply'])} in the west sector to {pc(CU['by_sector']['residential east']['lidar_night_over_exp_supply'])} in the east. Both measurements of demand before the lane are of the order of the supply that remains on one side today (about {one_side:.0f} places). On Terrebonne itself, {OF['pct_buildings']:.0f}\\,\\% of buildings and {OF['pct_dwellings']:.0f}\\,\\% of dwellings have a garage or driveway; the survey\\textquoteright{{}}s respondents on the street report an off-street place in {on['offstreet_built_ge1']} of {on['offstreet_built_answered']} households with a vehicle ({pc(on['offstreet_built_share'])}, interval {ci(on['offstreet_built_ci'])}), those within a block in {bl['offstreet_built_ge1']} of {bl['offstreet_built_answered']}. The survey\\textquoteright{{}}s {co['street_parkers']} street parkers and {co['hardship_strict']} reports of strict winter hardship are floors under a population of {RL['terrebonne']['dwellings']:,} dwellings on Terrebonne and {RL['within_block']['dwellings']:,} within a block, of which the survey reached {pc(RL['coverage']['on_terrebonne'])} and {pc(RL['coverage']['within_block'])}. The conditions the hardship questions describe occurred on about {WN['mean_storm_to_loading_sector']:.0f} nights a winter over the last five, {WN['mean_loading_sector']:.0f} of them with snow loading. No measurement of present-day occupancy, with the lane in place, exists; the first parking analysis the borough has commissioned since 2022 is due on November 27, 2026."""

LAY = f"""Lay summary

The survey asked people who live on rue de Terrebonne or within a block of it about their parking: whether they keep a car, whether they have a place off the street, where the car was parked last night, and how hard winter parking has been. The answers are counts of the people who replied, not measurements of the street. This note sets them beside the measurements that do exist.

The borough's own 2023 study counted the parking places and measured how full they were on two days in the autumn of 2022. It found room everywhere: on the Thursday evening, half of the places on Terrebonne were empty, and so were about {tp['stubs_vacant']} places on the first 50 metres of the cross streets. A night-time laser survey of the city in 2015 shows {CU['lidar_night_by_eye']} cars parked along Terrebonne, about one place in three, fewer in the west and more in the east. Both of those counts, taken before the bike lane, are close to the number of places that remain on the one side of the street that still has parking today.

Seven in ten homes on Terrebonne have a garage or a driveway, by a reading of the 1954 insurance plans and of today's aerial imagery. Survey respondents living on Terrebonne reported one less often, about half of the households with a car; respondents within a block reported one about as often as the imagery shows. The difference for the street itself is within what a sample of 35 can show, but it suggests that households without off-street parking were more likely to answer.

{co['street_parkers']} respondents from the corridor had parked on a street the night before, {co['parked_terrebonne']} of them on Terrebonne itself, and {co['hardship_strict']} described real difficulty in winter under the survey's strict definition. Those are floors: there are {RL['terrebonne']['dwellings']:,} dwellings on Terrebonne and about {RL['within_block']['dwellings']:,} within a block, and the survey reached one home in fifteen on the street and one in seventy nearby. The conditions the difficulty questions describe, snow removal pending or under way, happened on about {WN['mean_storm_to_loading_sector']:.0f} nights a winter over the last five winters, {WN['mean_loading_sector']:.0f} of them nights of snow loading itself, with stretches of three weeks or more without any every winter.

What no one has measured is how full the street is now, with the lane in place and parking on one side. The borough's own study of 2023 is the last measurement of occupancy; the next, commissioned in October 2026, is due on November 27 and counts the supply only.
"""
LAY_SHORT = f"""Lay summary

The survey asked people living on rue de Terrebonne or within a block about their parking. They count the people who replied, not the street. This note sets them beside the measurements on record: the borough's 2023 study (places and their occupancy on two days in 2022), a 2015 night laser survey, the 1954 insurance plans with today's aerial imagery, the City's roll of dwellings, and five winters of snow-loading records.

The 2023 study found half of Terrebonne's places empty on the Thursday evening; the 2015 night count found {CU['lidar_night_by_eye']} cars along the street, one place in three. Both, taken before the lane, are close to today's supply on one side. Seven in ten homes on Terrebonne have a garage or driveway; respondents on the street reported one less often, within what 35 answers can show. The survey's {co['street_parkers']} street parkers and {co['hardship_strict']} reports of winter difficulty are floors under {RL['terrebonne']['dwellings']:,} dwellings on the street and {RL['within_block']['dwellings']:,} nearby; snow loading constrained parking on about {WN['mean_storm_to_loading_sector']:.0f} nights a winter, {WN['mean_loading_sector']:.0f} loading nights. Nobody has measured how full the street is now, lane in place.
"""
open(os.path.join(HERE, 'lay_abstract_en_long.txt'), 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(LAY.strip() + '\n'); open(os.path.join(HERE, 'lay_abstract_en_short.txt'), 'w', encoding='utf-8').write(LAY_SHORT.strip() + '\n')
wc = lambda t: len(t.split()); lim = wc(' '.join(LAY.strip().split('\n\n')[1:3])); short_wc = wc(' '.join(LAY_SHORT.strip().split('\n\n')[1:]))
assert short_wc <= lim, f'short lay summary {short_wc} words exceeds {lim}'; print(f'lay summary: long {wc(LAY)} words, short {short_wc} (limit {lim})')
LAY_TEX = '\\section*{Lay summary}\n' + '\n\n'.join(esc(p) for p in LAY_SHORT.strip().split('\n\n')[1:]) + '\n'

